      "content": "The High Court in *Vaughan v. Maritime Logistics Ltd [2022]* held that the defendant breached the duty of care under the Merchant Shipping Act 1995 by failing to maintain traction sensors on cargo vessels, a failure directly attributable to the general principle of neighborliness from *Donoghue v Stevenson [1932]* and the three-stage test for foreseeability and proximity established in *Caparo Industries plc v Dickman [1990]*. Furthermore, the court applied the reasoning from *Robinson v Chief Constable of West Yorkshire Police [2018]* to confirm that existing precedents must be rigorously applied to modern negligence duties, while also drawing on the specific maritime liability standards set forth in *The Muncaster Castle [1961]* regarding the strict obligation to maintain cargo safety equipment and the carrier's non-negotiable duty to ensure the seaworthiness of the vessel as reinforced in *The Eurymedon [1975]*.",
